  tools   : tools/hostsim.py on tb277 eth3 -- answers ARP and ICMP echo for a list
            of addresses from a fake MAC, can be silenced for selected addresses on
            a timer, and writes one timestamped line per echo request it receives
            (`--log-echo`), which is the record every interval below is read from;
            tools/arpleg.py for the sniff-only and crafted-frame legs;
            tools/poll.py sampling the DUT's own `show ping-poll brief` every 2 s;
            tools/qmark.py for the read-only `?` probes; tools/cfg.py to send
            configuration; tools/ckcon.py and tools/ckyn.py to drive the console
            and answer its (y/n) prompts. All on tb277, through the tools/tb
            wrapper.

  Measurement stance: STANDING-ORDERS §7 requires crafted frames rather than ping,
  because a ping cannot select what it sends. This case is the exception, and
  deliberately so: the frames being graded are the DUT's OWN ICMP echo requests,
  emitted by its ping-poll process. They cannot be crafted by the tester, so they
  are graded where they land -- on the wire at the host NIC, by an instrument that
  only listens and answers. Three independent records cover every measured leg: the
  host's per-request log, the DUT's sampled state, and the DUT's own log and debug
  output. In every counted leg, ping-poll instance 2 (-> 10.38.22.45) is the in-leg
  instrument control and is NEVER silenced: whenever instance 1's traffic is
  expected to stop, instance 2's must still be counted, so a zero for instance 1 is
  a measurement and not a dead capture.

